comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-26T00:21:26.298Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check: complete all-status3118-item corpus plus fresh keyword AND and hybrid linked-test command/replacement/recovery searches and open/in_progress inventories; full live pm-9djr6d and pm-bjpo lineage retained. This is distinct refusal recovery UX. Intake only: open and unclaimed."
  "2026-11-01T21:06:25.972Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation (src/sdk/test/update.ts): a command or cmd key in --set-json or in the SDK patch is refused with linked_test_command_immutable and changed:false, under the item lock and before any write. When the patch names a new non-empty command, recovery.suggested_retry_args is one scope-preserving invocation: pm --pm-path <tracker> test <id> --remove command=<exact current command> --add-json <entry>. The removal falls back to --remove-index <locked position> when the entry has no unique command. The entry keeps the selected test's editable metadata, overlaid with the patch's other fields, and gets the new command. That is the existing atomic remove+add path: one tests_add history event, fresh provenance, other entries untouched, replacement appended at the end. Repeating the current command or sending an empty one gets the same code with a pointer to the editable fields instead. Other unsupported keys (provenance, editor stamps, unknown keys, the timeout alias) now refuse with linked_test_patch_fields_unsupported, with recovery.allowed_values listing the editable fields and field naming the first offender. The message text is unchanged.\n\nProof (tests/integration/linked-tests/linked-test-editing.integration.spec.ts): the SDK and CLI refusals leave bytes unchanged. Executing the CLI's advertised retry replaces only the selected entry (the untouched entry deep-equals its prior state), adds exactly one tests_add event, and history --verify --strict-exit passes. The same-command, empty-command, unsupported-field, empty-patch and legacy path-only (index fallback) cases are covered too. MCP and PmClient share the same SDK function.\n"
  "2026-11-01T21:46:36.711Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 1 on PR #1649. Two findings on this item, both verified and fixed:\n- Identity collision: if the replacement has the same identity (command, path, scope, context modes) as another entry, the add path deduplicates it away, so the advertised retry would delete the selected entry and add nothing. The refusal now detects this using linkedTestsHaveSameIdentity (moved to entry.ts and shared with the add path), offers no retry, and names the colliding entry's --update-index and the selected entry's --remove-index.\n- Silently dropped companion fields: the rest of the patch is validated before a retry is built. Unsupported keys, scope:null and invalid values (parsed exactly as --add-json parses them) now refuse instead of being dropped.\nThe integration spec covers the collision, unsupported key, scope:null and negative timeout cases, each with unchanged bytes.\n"
  "2026-11-01T22:35:05.014Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 2 on PR #1649: three findings on this item, all verified and fixed.\n- Conflicting command/cmd values collapsed silently: they now refuse, matching the add parser. Equal aliases are still accepted.\n- A replacement invoking pm test-all got a retry the add path would refuse. The recursion guard (assertNoRecursiveTestAllCommand and its helpers) moved from execution.ts to linked-command-detection.ts so update.ts can share it without an import cycle; the refusal now carries that reason and no retry.\n- The retry dropped explicit controls. --author, --message and --force from the refused edit are now appended to the retry, so ownership and provenance match the request.\nThe command-edit refusal is split into resolveRequestedCommand, admitReplacementEntry and replacementControlArgs to stay under the complexity cap.\n"
  "2026-11-01T23:05:40.754Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit full review on PR #1649 (head f7e12f69b): fixed an own scope: undefined in the patch overriding the selected scope (undefined now means not supplied; only null clears), and the SDK retry hint now names the global/tracker argument. The alias-conflict and author/force findings were already fixed in round 2. Two concurrency findings, removal binding and identity re-check between refusal and retry, need an atomic replacement primitive under one item lock and are filed as pm-5igljl. LINKED_TEST_EDITS.md notes the separate-mutation race.\n"
